    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most players spend weeks choosing a paddle and seconds choosing a ball, then wonder why the game feels different at every court. The ball is half the equation. An outdoor ball with 40 small holes plays fast and cuts through wind; an indoor ball with 26 larger holes plays slower and softer on a gym floor. Hard outdoor balls crack in the cold, soft ones go out of round, and only some of them are approved for sanctioned play. This guide picks six balls that cover the real situations, and explains the specs that separate them.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started from the USA Pickleball equipment standards, because they define what a legal ball is and give a fixed reference for every spec on a packet. A conforming ball must weigh between 0.78 and 0.935 ounces, measure 2.87 to 2.97 inches across, have between 26 and 40 circular holes, and rebound 30 to 34 inches when dropped from 78 inches onto a granite surface. Every approved ball sits inside those limits, so the differences that matter are where in the range each ball lands, and how it is built.
          </p>

          <p>
            We then looked at what players actually complain about. The complaints fall into three groups: balls that crack too soon, balls that play too fast or too slow for the group, and balls that behave differently in wind or cold. Each pick here answers one of those problems rather than chasing a single best ball, because there is no single best ball. A gym league, a windy public court and a winter morning game need different things.
          </p>

          <p>
            We verified specs against manufacturer pages and major pickleball retailers, and where sources disagree, we say so rather than picking the number that sounds best. Weight is the most common disagreement, because some listings round and others quote a different version of the ball. We did not invent crack rates, durability hours or test results. Where a feature is a manufacturer claim, such as a ball flying straighter in wind, it is labelled as a claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Approval status was the last filter. Five of the six picks are listed as USA Pickleball approved, and the one that is not is included deliberately as a practice ball, with its status stated plainly. The approved list changes several times a year, so if you are buying for a sanctioned event, check the current list on the USA Pickleball site before you order a bulk pack.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="holes-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Hole Count and Weight
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hole count is the quickest way to tell an outdoor ball from an indoor one. Outdoor balls typically have around 40 smaller holes, which reduces drag and keeps the ball flying straighter in wind. Indoor balls typically have around 26 larger holes, which slow the ball down and give a softer, more controllable feel on a smooth gym floor. The rules allow anything from 26 to 40, so some balls sit in between, like the 38-hole Selkirk Pro S1.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hole size and pattern matter as well as the count. The Dura Fast 40 uses two sizes of hole, and Selkirk uses a patented pattern of varied sizes, both with the aim of keeping the flight consistent regardless of how the ball is struck. Evenly spaced, cleanly drilled holes are what make a ball fly true. A ball with rough or uneven holes can wobble in the air, which is one reason cheap no-name balls feel unpredictable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight affects pace and wind resistance. Outdoor balls tend to sit near the top of the legal range, around 26 grams, so they carry through a breeze. Indoor balls are often a little lighter, like the Gamma Photon Indoor listed at about 24 grams, which suits the slower indoor game. A heavier ball also hits harder, which is part of why outdoor play feels faster and why paddle choice matters more outdoors, as our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">pickleball paddle guide</a> explains.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
