          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an Indoor Ball on an Outdoor Court</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Indoor balls are lighter with larger holes, so wind pushes them around and gritty surfaces wear them quickly. The game turns slow and unpredictable. Keep at least one approved outdoor ball for outdoor courts, and save indoor balls for gyms and sheltered practice where they belong.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Balls in a Cold Car</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hard outdoor balls become more brittle as they cool, so a ball that sat in a freezing car overnight is far more likely to crack in the first game. Store spares indoors at room temperature and warm a ball in your pocket before play on a cold morning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Bulk Pack Without Trying the Ball</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hundred balls is a big commitment if your group finds them too fast or they crack in your weather. Buy a small pack first, play with it for a couple of weeks, and only then order in bulk once you know the ball suits your court and conditions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Every Ball Is Tournament Legal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Only balls on the current USA Pickleball approved list can be used in sanctioned events, and the list changes during the year. Some popular balls, like the Onix Pure 2 Outdoor, are not approved. Check the current list before practising for or playing in an event.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Playing On With a Dead or Cracked Ball</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cracked ball or one that has gone out of round produces dead bounces and odd flights that ruin rallies. Drop-test any ball that feels off, retire it to the practice bucket if it bounces low, and throw away any ball with a visible crack.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
