            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between indoor and outdoor pickleball balls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Outdoor balls usually have around 40 smaller holes, a harder shell and weigh near the top of the legal range, so they fly straight in wind and survive rough courts. Indoor balls usually have around 26 larger holes and a softer feel, which suits smooth gym floors. Using the wrong type changes pace and control noticeably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do pickleball balls crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Outdoor balls are made from hard plastics that become more brittle as they cool, so cold weather is the most common cause of cracks. Seams are another weak point, which is why one-piece balls tend to last longer. Storing balls in a cold car and hitting them hard straight away makes cracking far more likely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What pickleball ball do the pros use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tournaments choose one approved ball per event, so the answer changes. The Franklin X-40 is the official ball of the US Open Pickleball Championships, per Franklin, and balls such as the Dura Fast 40 have long been used in competitive play. Check which ball your event uses and practise with it beforehand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many holes does a pickleball have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                USA Pickleball allows between 26 and 40 circular holes. Outdoor balls typically have around 40 smaller holes and indoor balls around 26 larger ones. Some balls sit in between, such as the 38-hole Selkirk Pro S1. Hole size and spacing matter as much as the count for a straight, consistent flight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do pickleball balls last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the ball, the court, the weather and how hard your group hits. Hard outdoor balls can crack within a few sessions in cold weather, while softer and one-piece balls generally last longer. Track the date you open a pack and when balls fail to learn what to expect at your court.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are USA Pickleball approved balls better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Approval confirms that a ball meets the official size, weight, hole and bounce standards and can be used in sanctioned events. It is not a quality ranking, and approved balls still differ in pace and durability. For casual play an unapproved ball is fine, but approved balls are generally more consistent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best pickleball ball for cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Softer outdoor balls, such as the Penn 40, are generally less prone to cracking in the cold than the hardest balls, and the Selkirk Pro S1 carries a registered no-crack warranty. Whatever you use, keep balls at room temperature before play and warm them in a pocket on chilly mornings.
              </p>
            </div>

